  Identical hashes are copies. A crate with its own hash typed its own.

CONTROL at least one of the four is independent : 1 (want >= 1)
CONTROL warm-container-shared is independent    : 1 (want 1)

VERDICT: the four packed-end crates are not four independent instances.
         Three of the four depend on bitpack-plan-shared, whose own
         PlanColumn/MacColumn are the same column-and-no-element shape,
         and the copied SplitMix64 and Plan13 alias show a shared
         ancestor independent of the cargo graph. Counting the family
         once, the independent instances supporting 154 F6 are:
           1. warm-container-shared's Carrier: Copy bound (structural)
              -- and note SplitMix64 is in 6 of 6, warm-container-shared
              included, so its VOCABULARY is template-shared too. What
              supports F6 there is the Copy bound, which is a fact about
              the target rather than about the template, so the sharing
              does not touch it.
           2. bitpack-shared (bench-core only)
           3. the bitpack-plan-shared family, as ONE instance
         Three, which meets RULES.md's bar. Not five.

AND SECTION D CLOSES THE OBVIOUS OBJECTION: SplitMix64 is published, so
         its presence in six crates could be six people reading one paper.
         It is not: five of the six bodies are byte-identical once
         whitespace is removed, and the sixth is bitpack-shared, which is
         also the one crate with no bitpack dependency. The copying and
         the independence agree with each other.

GENERALISES: SplitMix64 is declared identically in 6 of 6 crates examined.
         Every bench crate in this corpus descends from one template, so
         'N bench crates agree' is worth much less than N anywhere in this
         panel, not only here. A vocabulary count over these crates is one
         instance unless the dependency graph and the copied symbols are
         checked first.
